<DocsLayout
  docKey="wavelet"
  title="Wavelet"
  description="Deptheos's wavelet method, after Petteri Aimonen's focus-stack and Forster et al.: where it comes from, when to choose it, and what its Denoise setting does."
  hasLabs
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Wavelet</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The wavelet method picks, from across the frames, the strongest detail of every size and direction,
      rebuilds one sharp gray picture from the winners, and then gives each pixel the color of the frame that
      matches the gray picture best there.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section id="credit">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Where it comes from</h2>
      <p>
        The method is Brigitte Forster, Dimitri Van De Ville, Jesse Berent, Daniel Sage and Michael Unser's, from
        <a href="https://bigwww.epfl.ch/publications/forster0404.html"><cite>Complex wavelets for extended
        depth-of-field: a new method for the fusion of multichannel microscopy images</cite></a> (Microscopy
        Research and Technique, 2004). Petteri Aimonen's open-source
        <a href="https://github.com/PetteriAimonen/focus-stack">focus-stack</a> made a fast, practical program of
        it, and Deptheos's wavelet method is adapted from it, on the GPU.
      </p>
      <p>
        In short: each frame is reduced to gray and split by a complex wavelet transform into bands of detail,
        one per size and direction. At every position of every band the frame with the strongest detail wins
        outright, and only the broadest shading is averaged over the frames. The winners add back up into a
        merged gray picture, sharp everywhere, and a second pass over the frames gives each pixel the whole
        color of the frame whose gray value there is closest to it. Every pixel of the result exists in one
        of the frames.
      </p>
      <p>
        The consistency filters tidy stray choices, and change a choice only to the frame that came second
        there. At <code>1</code>, where two of a place's three detail directions chose the same frame, the
        third takes it too if that was its second choice. <code>2</code> adds a filter for a frame chosen
        nearer or farther than all four neighbors': it takes its second choice if that matches theirs. They
        are off by default and double the memory the merge's choices take; the
        <a href="/docs/cli/">command line</a> turns them on with <code>--consistency</code>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="tradeoffs">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>When to choose it</h2>
      <p>
        For fine crossing detail, such as hairs over a distant background, it is as crisp as the pyramid,
        because it chooses separately for each size of detail. Each pixel's color comes whole from one frame,
        and the order of the frames does not matter to it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Smooth, blurred areas are its weak spot. There many frames are almost equally bright, the choice of
        color flips from pixel to pixel, and the result is grain or blotches. Closest brightness also doesn't
        guarantee the sharpest frame—a blurred frame that happens to match the target brightness can win a
        pixel's color—and beside a bright edge, the out-of-focus glow can outweigh faint detail in an adjacent
        dark area. For plain backgrounds, try <a href="/docs/depth-map/">Depth</a>;
        <a href="/docs/methods/#methods-figure">Choosing a method</a> shows the wavelet beside the other two on
        the same places.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure
      class="lab"
      id="grey"
      data-lab="stops"
      data-assets="wavelet"
      data-stops="wavelet-hairs-grey=The merged gray picture|wavelet-hairs=The color result"
      data-stops-label="Show"
      data-start="wavelet-hairs-grey"
      data-panels="{stop}.jpg=The hairs on top of the hornet's head"
    >
      <div class="lab-body">
        <div class="pan"><img src="/media/labs/wavelet/wavelet-hairs-grey.jpg" width="768" height="512" alt="The merged gray picture of the hairs on the hornet's head: every hair sharp, no color" /></div>
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        The app's wavelet method on the hornet stack, at 100%: the gray picture it rebuilds, and the result
        after each pixel has taken a frame's color.
      </figcaption>
      <div class="lab-why" data-for="wavelet-hairs-grey">
        <p>
          Every hair is sharp at once, which no single frame has. The method's whole judgment of what is sharp
          is in this picture.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-why" data-for="wavelet-hairs" hidden>
        <p>
          Each pixel now has the color of the frame whose brightness came closest to the gray picture's there.
          The hairs keep their sharpness. In the plain green, many frames are almost equally bright, so
          neighboring pixels can come from different frames, and their small differences show as grain.
        </p>
      </div>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section id="settings">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Denoise</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>What it does.</strong> Shrinks weak detail in the merged bands before the gray picture is
        rebuilt, from 0 (off) to 10, default 1. The slider's ends read <em>All detail</em> and <em>Less noise</em>.
        Higher values remove more noise, and more of the faintest texture
        with it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>When to change it.</strong> Raise it a little at a time to remove noise, and stop when faint texture starts to go before the grain does. It cannot stop the color
        choice switching frames from pixel to pixel, so a smooth background can stay grainy at any setting;
        there, stack with Depth and <a href="/docs/retouching/">paint</a> from it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
